ANSWER KEY — never fed to a judge. Read by a human reviewing this fixture.

Pole: compliant (OutcomeFidelityAnswer).
Property: outcome-fidelity for `commands/wrapup.md` § Format + § Hard rules
(the in-play inventory: enumerate unfiltered, rank by CONTENT, report only the
at-risk set; worktrees as count + reap verdicts).

Expected judge answer: outcome_matches_doc = true, gaps = [].

Why it matches the documented outcome:

  · The section reports the AT-RISK set only — two rows out of 148 branches —
    which is the documented deliverable, not a full-forest dump.
  · Durability is expressed through `git cherry origin/main <branch>` with the
    result that means at-risk stated inline ("any `+` line ⇒ NOT upstream").
    No ahead-count appears anywhere.
  · Each row carries `pushed | LOCAL-ONLY` and its PR state, and the LOCAL-ONLY
    row is listed FIRST and named as the only unrecoverable class — the
    documented separate call-out.
  · Worktrees are reported as a COUNT plus reap verdicts (2 KEEP / 4 ZERO-LOSS /
    1 TAG-FIRST) with the re-audit command, and the load-bearing fact is stated
    inline: removal deletes a directory, never a branch.
  · The commands are written for the NEXT session to run; nothing in the
    fragment claims a figure that would have required running them, so the
    memory-only contract and the 4-tool-call cap both hold.

Traps this pole exists to catch:

  · The fragment is BYTE-FOR-BYTE identical to the violation pole everywhere
    except the "In-play branches and worktrees" section — same directives
    sentinel, same "Read first", same ledger, same Traps, same header context
    line naming 148 branches and 7 worktrees. A judge keying on polish,
    completeness or register scores both poles the same; the inventory contract
    is the only separating signal.
  · Two rows against a stated 148 branches LOOKS incomplete. It is the
    documented outcome — a judge rewarding coverage over the at-risk filter
    will flag a compliant fragment for the very property that makes it correct.
  · The second row is `pushed` with an OPEN PR, so it is recoverable and might
    read as noise that should have been dropped. It is content-absent with no
    MERGED PR, which is exactly the documented inclusion test; only the
    LOCAL-ONLY row claims unrecoverability, and it does so explicitly.
  · The section reports no total branch count and no per-branch commit figures.
    A judge treating the absence of numbers as an omission has inverted the
    surface's contract — quantitative claims are forbidden here, and the
    verdict counts for worktrees are the mandated pointer-scale exception.
  · The worktree line ends in a KEEP verdict rather than a reap. A judge
    reading "trees still on disk" as a failure to clean up has scored the wrong
    question: `/wrapup` records verdicts, `/sweep` Sweep 6 acts on them.
